WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:05.080
The next two topics deal with operators,&nbsp;
which help perform calculations and check&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.080 --> 00:00:09.440
for conditions within a block of code.&nbsp;
For the first of these two topics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.440 --> 00:00:15.080
the emphasis is learning not so much the operators&nbsp;
themselves but the sequence of execution of these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.080 --> 00:00:21.040
operators. I will first take you through the list&nbsp;
of operators and their order. After doing that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.040 --> 00:00:25.360
the next topic emphasizes what these operators&nbsp;
do for calculations and comparisons within&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.360 --> 00:00:29.960
Python code. The six types of operators and&nbsp;
the order in which they are applied are:

00:00:31.080 --> 00:00:32.520
Arithmetic

00:00:32.520 --> 00:00:34.280
Containment

00:00:34.280 --> 00:00:36.360
Comparison

00:00:36.360 --> 00:00:38.320
Identity

00:00:38.320 --> 00:00:39.800
Logical

00:00:39.800 --> 00:00:41.600
Assignment

00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:47.560
Those six types are all listed as exam topics,&nbsp;
both for the order of execution and for how these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.560 --> 00:00:53.640
operators drive results. There is an order of&nbsp;
operations to follow within each type of operator.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.640 --> 00:01:01.120
Before we go any further, though, understand this:&nbsp;
parentheses supersede these six operators. Over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.120 --> 00:01:06.400
the next few videos, we will look at some examples&nbsp;
of how parentheses take priority. So that we can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:11.240
focus on these operators, I will cover these first&nbsp;
with mostly raw numbers and then incorporate how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.240 --> 00:01:17.520
these operators help build code for games.&nbsp;
Let’s now look at some code. Let’s look first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:23.880
at assignments, which set values to variables.&nbsp;
The order of assignments is dependent upon the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.880 --> 00:01:33.800
order in which they are introduced within code.&nbsp;
Looking at this 131-assignment file, x equals 5,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.800 --> 00:01:43.160
and y equals 3. Then, we have a print statement&nbsp;
on line 3 to print x and y, which, when I run,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.160 --> 00:01:51.760
prints 5 and 3. Let’s now look at how these&nbsp;
assignments can be sequenced. I’m going to,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.760 --> 00:02:00.640
on line 4, add a statement to make y equal x.&nbsp;
There are two principles to note here. First,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.640 --> 00:02:07.200
because y is being assigned another value later in&nbsp;
the code, it will take this new value over the old&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:16.880
value, three in this case. Secondly, when using&nbsp;
an assignment, the variable on the left side of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.880 --> 00:02:24.440
the equal sign is always set to the value on the&nbsp;
right. In this case, y is now equal to what x is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.440 --> 00:02:34.200
which is five and I will prove that by adding&nbsp;
a print statement on line 5: to print y and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.200 --> 00:02:39.840
running the code. It is important to understand&nbsp;
the sequence of assignments so that, at any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.840 --> 00:02:46.600
given time, one can assign the correct value to&nbsp;
a variable and determine the value of a variable.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.600 --> 00:02:53.520
Getting this process wrong leads to logic errors,&nbsp;
which can take valuable time to locate and fix.

